mes Mitchell",email:"j.mitchell@arctic.innovation-inc",role:"Lead Cryogenics Engineer",facility:"Innovation Inc Arctic Facility"},{name:"Sarah Chen",email:"s.chen@desert.innovation-inc",role:"Systems Integration Lead",facility:"Innovation Inc Desert Complex"},{name:"Dr. Robert Kim",email:"r.kim@iitpp.innovation-inc",role:"Chief Safety Officer",facility:"IITPP — Sector D"},{name:"Officer Yu",email:"yu@iitpp.innovation-inc",role:"Security Operations Lead",facility:"IITPP — Security Hub"}];function rg(u,i,o){const c=i.toLowerCase(),f=o.toLowerCase();switch(u.email){case"y.tanaka@iitpp.innovation-inc":return c.includes("meltdown")||c.includes("emergency")||f.includes("3500")?`ALERT ACKNOWLEDGED — Initiating emergency cooling protocols now.

Core temp must stay below 3000K. I've dispatched a team to recalibrate lasers 2 and 4. The safeguard system should engage automatically at 3200K.

Meet me in the Core Control Room.

— Dr. Yuki Tanaka`:c.includes("laser")||c.includes("alignment")?`Thanks for flagging this. Laser drift is within acceptable parameters but bears watching. I've scheduled a full recalibration cycle for 0600 tomorrow.

The new alignment algorithm should reduce drift by ~15%. I'll forward the specs.

— Dr. Yuki Tanaka`:`Received. Current core readings look stable at ~2180K. The power output has been steady at 850MW — exactly where we want it for sustained operations.

I'm running a few simulations on the new cooling configuration. Will share results at the 1500 briefing.

— Dr. Yuki Tanaka`;case"j.mitchell@arctic.innovation-inc":return c.includes("freeze")||c.includes("cold")||f.includes("cryo")?`Fascinating! We've actually been studying cryogenic effects on reactor cores at the Arctic Facility. If your core drops below 100K, the crystalline structure of the fuel rods could become... unpredictable.

I'm attaching our latest research paper on low-temperature reactor dynamics. Could be relevant to your freezedown scenario.

— Dr. James Mitchell
Arctic Facility, Innovation Inc`:c.includes("coolant")||c.includes("cooling")?`Your coolant system is impressive — we use a similar sodium-based design here. Our efficiency tops out at 96% in -40°C ambient conditions.

One thing we've found: cycling the pumps every 72 hours prevents cavitation. Might help your 94% number.

— Dr. James Mitchell`:`Greetings from the frozen north! Our reactor core is humming along at 1900K — cold weather definitely helps with thermal management.

Saw your latest readings on the shared dashboard. Looking good. Let's sync up on the quarterly cross-facility report.

— Dr. James Mitchell`;case"s.chen@desert.innovation-inc":return c.includes("tram")||c.includes("transport")?`Your tram system is on my radar — literally! We've been developing an AI-based scheduling optimizer that reduced our transit times by 22%. Happy to share the codebase.

The key is predicting peak demand periods. At IITPP, I'd guess your busiest times are shift changes and during emergency drills.

— Sarah Chen`:c.includes("storage")||c.includes("distribution")||c.includes("cube")?`Storage distribution is one of my favorite topics! We handle 3000+ cubes daily here. The trick is batching deliveries by sector and using predictive restocking based on historical usage patterns.

Your barrel delivery system is elegant — the pneumatic tubes are a nice touch.

— Sarah Chen`:`Integration update: all three facilities are now synced on the shared monitoring API. I can see your reactor readings from here in the desert!

Also — love the new ASAS voice pack. Very... authoritative.

— Sarah Chen`;case"r.kim@iitpp.innovation-inc":return c.includes("safeguard")||c.includes("safety")||c.includes("emergency")?`SAFETY PROTOCOL ACKNOWLEDGED.

The safeguard system last passed diagnostics at 0900 today. Success probability scales inversely with core temperature: 95% below 2500K, dropping to 60% at 3200K, and 30% at 3500K.

Remember: safeguard activation requires BOTH keys — Core Control AND Facility Control. No exceptions.

— Dr. Robert Kim, Chief Safety Officer`:c.includes("radiation")||f.includes("μSv")?`Noted. Current radiation levels (3.2 μSv/h) are well within safe limits. The containment shield is rated for up to 50 μSv/h sustained.

That said, please ensure all personnel in Sector C are wearing their dosimeters. Quarterly readings are due next week.

— Dr. Robert Kim`:`Weekly safety report is attached. All systems nominal. The fire suppression test in Sector B went well — response time was under 12 seconds.

Reminder: monthly evacuation drill is scheduled for Friday at 1400.

— Dr. Robert Kim`;case"yu@iitpp.innovation-inc":return c.includes("zombie")||c.includes("outbreak")||c.includes("infection")?`ZOMBIE PROTOCOL ENGAGED.

Containment teams are being dispatched. Decontamination chambers are active. Manual turrets are armed.

Current infection rate: ${Math.floor(Math.random()*15+5)}%. Stay in a secure location and await further instructions.

— Officer Yu, Security Operations`:c.includes("security")||c.includes("breach")||c.includes("intruder")?`Security alert received. I'm reviewing the camera feeds now.

Perimeter sensors show no breaches. All access doors are secured. I'll send a patrol to verify Sector C.

Keep me posted.

— Officer Yu`:`All quiet on the security front. Patrols are running on schedule. Camera feeds are clear.

One thing: the motion sensor in the Turbine Hall triggered at 0330 — turned out to be a loose panel. Maintenance has been notified.

— Officer Yu`;default:return`Thank you for your message. I'll review and respond shortly.

